import Mathlib

/-!
# The DASHI digit function and its exact period

Lean mirror of the upstream Agda module `CRTPeriod.agda`, carried over through
the `agda2lean` mirror-fixture discipline (`RequestProject/Mirror/Fixtures.lean`).

The Agda module defines the *digit function*

  `d N = ((N % 71) + (N % 59) + (N % 47)) % 10`

and the constant `period = 71 * 59 * 47 = 196883`, and proves one theorem,
`period-thm : ∀ N k → d (N + k * period) ≡ d N`.  That direction is the easy
half: `period` is divisible by each of the three moduli, so each summand is
unchanged and therefore so is their sum mod `10`.

What the Agda does *not* say is whether `196883` is the *smallest* period, and
that is not obvious: `d` post-composes the Chinese-remainder triple
`N ↦ (N % 71, N % 59, N % 47)` — whose minimal period really is `196883` — with
the lossy map `(a, b, c) ↦ (a + b + c) % 10`, and a lossy map can collapse a
period.  Here it does not, and this module proves it.

`isPeriod_iff` is the sharp statement: the set of periods of `d` is *exactly*
the set of multiples of `196883`.  The proof is the standard subgroup argument
carried out in `ℕ` — the periods are closed under addition, under truncated
subtraction and hence under remainder, so they are closed under `gcd`
(`isPeriod_gcd`) — followed by three explicit refutations at `N = 0` for the
three maximal proper divisors `59·71`, `47·71`, `47·59` of `196883`
(`not_isPeriod_4189`, `not_isPeriod_3337`, `not_isPeriod_2773`).

The connection to `RequestProject/Mirror/Moonshine.lean` is `period_add_one`:
the exact period of the digit function is `196884 - 1 = 47 · 59 · 71`, the
dimension of the smallest faithful representation of the Monster, and the
`+1` that reaches the `j`-invariant coefficient is exactly the failure of `d`
to have any shorter period.
-/

